#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093324} :activity/id #uuid "708c0aaf-266b-5a9d-b352-10ed7b4b2883" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050644 :topic/id #uuid "5beaefa1-1d02-51a5-90bc-250adca28a57" :topic/math-academy-id 986} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093324 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093430 :tutorial/content "The equation of the tangent line to the curve $y = f(x)$ at the point $P(x_1,y_1),$ in point-slope form, is given by\n\n$$\ny - y_1 = m (x - x_1), \\hspace{.75cm} m=f'(x_1).\n$$\n\nHere, $m = f'(x_1)$ because $f'(x_1)$ represents the slope of the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ at the point $(x_1,y_1).$\n\nFor example, suppose we want to compute the equation of the tangent line to the curve $y=x^2$ at the point $P(1,1).$\n\n![](images/48/487d21289b468fb82efa2097e425dd560811dc3165890509dacbab8093e0ecdb.png)\n\nSince $y=x^2,$ we have $f(x)=x^2.$ We begin by calculating the derivative of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) \t\t = \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(x^2)= 2x\n$$\n\nThen, we determine the slope of the tangent at $P(1,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nm = f'(1)  = 2(1) = 2\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we substitute the slope $m=2$ and the coordinates of $P(1,1)$ into the point-slope form, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =2(x-1) \\\\ y & =2x-2+1 \\\\ y & =2x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the tangent is $y=2x-1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8b0ed179-00e3-5e1a-84fc-c78773cfef19" :tutorial/math-academy-id 354 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4a6af753-c85c-59f9-be67-03ca672b3493" :step/math-academy-id 657 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093325}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093325 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092382 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091951 :question/id #uuid "2f5afc63-6988-5676-b170-5826e659a5fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-144"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db808f48-6d0c-5b35-9342-b1817df65172" :knowledge-point/key-pr